  "note": "text_layer was null; every cell was read from the image. This is the top half of Table 2.6, printed on a lavender tint, with no body prose on the page. The table does not print configurations as strings with superscripts: each subshell is its own column and each cell holds the bare occupancy number, so the cells are transcribed as the plain digits printed, and an unoccupied subshell is an empty cell (\"\"). The header is read as 20 columns (Element, Z and the 18 subshell headings) even though the printed rules group the subshell headings by shell into six boxed bands (1s | 2s 2p | 3s 3p 3d | 4s 4p 4d 4f | 5s 5p 5d 5f | 6s 6p 6d 7s) and put Element and Z in one box; the subshell letters are printed in italic. Horizontal rules inside the table separate the period blocks (after He, after Ne, after Zn) and are structural only, so no blank rows were added for them. The 4f, 5d, 5f, 6s, 6p, 6d and 7s columns are empty for every row inside the box. EXCLUDED as outside target_box_in_crop (box lower edge y=1691): the table's final printed data row, which reads Xe | 54 | 1s 2 | 2s 2 | 2p 6 | 3s 2 | 3p 6 | 3d 10 | 4s 2 | 4p 6 | 4d 10 | 5s 2 | 5p 6, whose glyphs run y=1701-1715, and the table's closing rule at y=1734 - the box stops 2 px below the bottom of the \"I\" (Z = 53) row, so the table as transcribed ends at iodine and is one row short of the table as printed. Also excluded: the cyan title above the box, \"Table 2.6  Electronic Configurations of the Elements\"; the footnote below the box, \"* Elements with exceptional electronic configurations\", which is what the asterisks on Cr*, Cu*, Nb*, Mo*, Ru*, Rh*, Pd* and Ag* refer to; the page number 66 and the running head CHEMISTRY; and the diagonal \"(C) NCERT not to be republished\" watermark crossing the table."
